#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153118} :activity/id #uuid "29345f23-5e83-5b04-afba-830c11338a90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041 :topic/id #uuid "7c0a3263-cc36-57b6-839b-6ff5594cae61" :topic/math-academy-id 2678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153387 :tutorial/content "A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is an **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in X,$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ then $x_1=x_2.$\n\nWe can read the above statement as follows:\n\n*The function $f$ that maps from the domain $X$ to the codomain $Y$ is an injection if, for all $x_1$ and $x_2$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f(x_1)$ and $f(x_2)$ are equal, then $x_1$ and $x_2$ are equal*\n\nIn other words, a function is an injection if no two different elements of the domain map to the same element of the range.\n\nFor example, the following diagram shows an example of an injection.\n\n![](images/43/437dc5ed10f647d2a216ca1ce16aeedd007063f473e0fb9aa8e72b58f22fa292.png)\n\nHowever, the following is **not** an injection because there are two different elements in the domain ($1$ and $2$) that map to the same element of the range ($-1$).\n\n![](images/2d/2d9e2540f949ad6ee3daa6c8236342a9e4b7c6349402c55c79c71be67162df48.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f4bd79b-5ff6-5d9c-ab61-78957d53057b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4194 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49c27874-4216-5f46-87e3-47eed303d536" :step/math-academy-id 25449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152004 :question/id #uuid "4fa1e488-75e9-5da8-9a07-c1fa4c797531" :question/math-academy-id "e-6869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050318}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385698}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "9403daa8-8f39-5ff4-a903-7b35be698e09" :step/math-academy-id 25450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :question/id #uuid "9898b130-1ef8-5c59-a6de-dae8640155ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-6871"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385658}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"} :step/id #uuid "789f00d2-d056-5151-913d-3ecb5a85b5ed" :step/math-academy-id 25452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153396 :tutorial/content "How do we determine whether a function $f$ is an injection without using a graph or mapping diagram?\n\nTo do this, we take $x_1$ and $x_2$ (arbitrary but fixed) in the domain of $f,$ and we construct the equation $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$ Using algebraic manipulation, we aim to get some relation between the variables $x_1$ and $x_2.$ If we get $x_1=x_2,$ this implies that $f$ is an injection.\n\nLet's use this idea to prove that $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow (0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=2^x$ is an injection.\n\nFirst, we construct the equation $f(x_1) = f(x_2).$ This gives $$\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using algebraic manipulation, we reduce this relationship to $x_1=x_2,$ as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, for any $x_1, x_2 \\in\\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ we have $x_1=x_2.$ In other words, $f$ is an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "6592b1a8-cfe6-5f38-be26-79e350169bf6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4229 :tutorial/title "Identifying Injections Defined on Arbitrary Sets"} :step/id #uuid "186077d7-1922-5507-80c3-e962b24bfb61" :step/math-academy-id 25650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152202 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152009 :question/id #uuid "0d641eb0-a77d-5c36-a232-8a03486ddd49" :question/math-academy-id "e-6949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385719}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "557e7c85-65bb-549c-ae5d-8e48fdaa9be7" :step/math-academy-id 25651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152010 :question/id #uuid "98e5e792-4ff6-5555-af05-b3a7e4655053" :question/math-academy-id "e-6950"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385733}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "216a5b7d-dc96-5cff-97b4-eea712552a91" :step/math-academy-id 25652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153399 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that $Y$ is a relatively small finite set, and we want to know if a function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an injection. All we need to do is go through each element $x$ in $X$ ensure that they each map to *distinct* elements of $Y.$\n\nFor example, consider the function $f: \\{-1,0,1\\} \\to \\{{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{red}4}\\}$ defined by $f(x) =4x^2.$ Is it an injection?\n\nSince the domain $X$ is a small, finite set, it's easy to compute the range.\n\n$$f(-1)={\\color{red}4},\\quad f(0)={\\color{blue}0}, \\quad f(1)={\\color{red}4}.$$\n\nNote that $f(-1) = f(1) = {\\color{red}4},$ but $-1\\neq 1.$ So, the elements in the domain $X$ do not map to distinct elements in $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $f$ is not an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "339a2c21-49d8-5fc7-b2ba-5d9dbcb242dc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4309 :tutorial/title "Identifying Injections When the Domain is a Small Finite Set"} :step/id #uuid "f9438c8c-4c2e-5c84-bef4-cdbef3e05650" :step/math-academy-id 26197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153400 :tutorial/content "When a function is presented as a graph, there is a useful graphical method to determine whether a function is an injection or not. This method is called the **horizontal line test.**\nHere is how it works:\n\n**Step 1.** Plot the graph of the function.\n\n**Step 2.** Draw a horizontal line on the plot.\n\n**Step 3.** Imagine moving the horizontal line up and down across the entire range.\n\n- If the horizontal line hits the graph only once no matter where we draw it, then the function is an injection. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- If the horizontal line *ever* hits the function more than once, then the function is *not* an injection. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use the horizontal line test to determine whether the following function is an injection.\n\n![](images/77/77a23681c6f0f2bf4623017bafe70ca99f80eef9d7535188e2427bfd10a93035.png)\n\nIf we draw any horizontal line on the plot of the function above, it will hit the graph only once, no matter where we place the line.\n\n![](images/a9/a9afda7b5db1062bdb78d81c998def5deee0c118015e1c0486197f6460597ef0.png)\n\nTherefore, this function is an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "eef22729-9c3b-5d13-bc5f-e473bb1cb189" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4314 :tutorial/title "The Horizontal Line Test"} :step/id #uuid "fccf2e16-8be5-596c-a284-ffed9d43209b" :step/math-academy-id 26215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153128}}] :activity/title "Injections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152351}]}
